<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tlaxcalans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tlaxcalan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tlaxcalans were the people of Tlaxcala, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> confederation east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> that remained independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. They were long-standing enemies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> and fought repeated ritual wars with them. When the Spanish arrived in 1519 the Tlaxcalans became their most important allies, providing thousands of warriors for the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans.jpg" alt="Tlaxcalans" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilhelm Sievers (1860 - 1921) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aztec_Indians_Mexico_Tlaxcalan_Cortez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…peoples: by some estimates Cortés's army at the final siege numbered as many as 100,000 warriors, of whom fewer than 1,000 were Spaniards; the great majority were <b>Tlaxcalans</b>, Huexotzincas, Chalcans, Texcocan factions and others who seized the opportunity to destroy the power that had dominated or threatened them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Hernán Cortés landed on the Gulf coast in 1519, founded Veracruz, and with the decisive help of indigenous allies, above all the <b>Tlaxcalans</b>, besieged Tenochtitlan, which fell on 13 August 1521 after a smallpox epidemic had already devastated its defenders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The patron god of the <b>Tlaxcalans</b> was Camaxtli, a god of the hunt and of war closely linked to Mixcoatl, although finds at Tepeticpac suggest that Tezcatlipoca was also prominent there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Mexico, Cortés's victory depended on tens of thousands of indigenous allies, above all the <b>Tlaxcalans</b>, who had their own reasons to destroy the Mexica tribute state.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
